1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bernadine, at age 14, went to live with Aunt Lizzy, Elizabeth Brinkman Weiss(sister to our grandmother Theresia) in the city.  Bernadine, at 14, decided that she had to get a job.  She put on Aunt Lizzy's hat and w
2 CONC ent for an interview.  When they asked how old she was, she said 17.  She got the job in an office.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are some deductions from this story.  Aunt Lizzy was ostracised for having married that 'city boy', Joe Weiss, catcher for the Tigers.   When she offered refuge for Bernadine, she may have redeemed herself.  Bernadine relate
2 CONC d this story with much regret for having lied about her age--and then went off to the convent!  Must we think that the circumstances at home were dire to promote this action.  At this time siblings William, Dolores and Marie were 
2 CONC all under 6 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Dueweke:  This might have been when they lived on the Dohney farm on the south side of Ten Mile Road about half way between Beech and Inkster. Dad said in his book, "When Loretta [she married Mike Rumenapp and died of a strok
2 CONC e when she was six months pregnant on 1/27/1928] was 16, she worked in a factory in Detroit during World War I.  I think Helen was doing house work in Detroit at age 14 or 15.  Bernadine started working in an office for the Grand 
2 CONC Trunk Western Railroad at 14 and worked 3 years.  Then she entered the convent at age 17 in 1923."
